> Avro to become the best way to serialize for Hadoop.  I recognize that
> not all of Hadoop
> has been bound to Avro yet, but I am not current on the details;  I
> assume Avro
> is still the long term strategic direction.  True?

Best asked on the Avro mailing list.

>
> We could contribute to an effort to make RHIPE more Avro friendly, if
> others are interested.
>

Since I have stumbled and paused on writing a splittable input format
(like SequenceFiles) based on protocol buffers (i.e can be created in
other languages unlike ProtocolBuffers), I'm happy to see Avro used.

However, Protocol Buffers is used extensively and I haven't wrapped
the PB api calls. PB is not just used for the streaming data
interchange, but also between R and the Java server. In summary, it's
very much all over the place.

I appreciate the time you have taken to ask these questions.

Cheers
Saptarshi

From uc47f732bf0f6ef00 at groups.google.com  Tue Aug 23 00:07:21 2011
From: uc47f732bf0f6ef00 at groups.google.com (masayang)
Date: Tue, 23 Aug 2011 00:07:21 +0000
Subject: bootstrap script for 0.66 on Amazon Elastic MapReduce
Message-ID: <oRmJ61NtnQM.rBQTRow3M1oJ@rhipe.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rhipe/c/oRmJ61NtnQM/m/rBQTRow3M1oJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I updated the bootstrap script for 0.66.

--begin
#! /bin/bash
set -e

export HADOOP=/home/hadoop

SOURCESLIST=sources.list
APTDIR=/etc/apt
CP=/bin/cp
SUDO=/usr/bin/sudo
APT=/usr/bin/apt-get
WGET=/usr/bin/wget
TAR=/bin/tar
MAKE=/usr/bin/make
R=/usr/bin/R
LDCONFIG=/sbin/ldconfig
CAT=/bin/cat
CHOWN=/bin/chown
LIBR=/usr/local/lib/R
LIBDIR=/usr/lib
GFORTRAN=/usr/lib/gcc/x86_64-linux-gnu/4.3/libgfortran*

PROTOBUF_PATH=http://protobuf.googlecode.com/files/
PROTOBUF=protobuf-2.4.1
PROTOBUF_SRC=$PROTOBUF.tar.gz

RHIPE_PATH=http://ml.stat.purdue.edu/rhipe/download/
RHIPE_SRC=Rhipe_0.66.tar.gz

$CAT << EOF > $SOURCESLIST
deb http://http.us.debian.org/debian   stable         main contrib non-
free
deb http://security.debian.org         lenny/updates  main contrib non-
free
deb http://security.debian.org         stable/updates main contrib non-
free
EOF

$SUDO $CP $SOURCESLIST $APTDIR/$SOURCESLIST
$SUDO $APT update
$SUDO $APT -y install r-base-dev
$SUDO $APT -y install pkg-config
$SUDO $APT -y install emacs ess

$SUDO $CP $GFORTRAN $LIBDIR

$WGET $PROTOBUF_PATH$PROTOBUF_SRC
$TAR xvfz $PROTOBUF_SRC
cd $PROTOBUF && ./configure && $MAKE && $SUDO $MAKE install && $SUDO
$LDCONFIG
$WGET $RHIPE_PATH$RHIPE_SRC
$SUDO $CHOWN -R hadoop:hadoop $LIBR
$R CMD INSTALL $RHIPE_SRC

cd $HADOOP && $CAT << EOF >> .bashrc
export HADOOP=/home/hadoop
export HADOOP_BIN=/home/hadoop/bin
EOF

cd $HADOOP && $CAT << EOF >> install.R
install.packages("biglm", repos="http://cran.r-project.org/")
install.packages("multicore", repos="http://cran.r-project.org/")
EOF
$R CMD BATCH install.R
--end

How to use it?

First of all, you need Amazon Web Service account, then upload the
script onto your S3 bucket. You also need elastic-mapreduce command
line tools.

Following command will start Rhipe cluster with five m1.large
instances.

$ elastic-mapreduce --create --alive --bootstrap-action s3://<your
bucket here>/bootstrap_0.66.sh <http://bootstrap_0.66.sh> --num-instances 5 --instance-type
m1.large

The command will return the instance id like j-AABBCCDD. You can login
to the cluster using this id.

$ elastic-mapreduce --ssh j-AABBCCDD

>From here, you can start your R session.

Don't forget to terminate the cluster when you are done.

$ elastic-mapreduce --terminate j-AABBCCDD

Enjoy.

Masa Nakamura

